<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Akhmat Khan</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1481">1481</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Ahmed Khan bin Kuchuk, Ahmed Khan bin Küchük</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Akhmat Khan was khan of the Great Horde, the main successor of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-horde">Golden Horde</a>, from about 1465 to 1481. He demanded tribute from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> and allied with Poland-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivan-iii">Ivan III</a>. In 1480 his army faced the Russians across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ugra-river">Ugra River</a> but withdrew without battle, an event taken as the end of Mongol overlordship in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>. He was killed the next year.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/akhmat-khan.jpg" alt="Akhmat Khan" width="320" height="227" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Aleksey Kivshenko · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kivshenko_Ivan_III_tears_off_the_khans_missive_letter.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
